INT. FAIRLANES BOWLING ALLEY - PINSETTER ROOM - NIGHT

Behind the lanes. A long steel cavern of dead machines, each one frozen mid-cycle. Dust. The smell of oil and old wax.

SHORTY ALVARADO, 50s, wide, slow, in a faded satin bowling jacket, sits on an upturned crate. A duffel bag between his boots.

Footsteps on the metal catwalk.

TANYA BOSCO, 30s, leather jacket, no smile, walks the length of the room. A flashlight in her hand, not lit.

SHORTY
You're late, Tanya.

TANYA
Traffic.

SHORTY
At two in the morning.

TANYA
There's always traffic, Shorty.

She stops ten feet away. Looks at the duffel.

TANYA (CONT'D)
That it?

SHORTY
That's it.

TANYA
Open it.

SHORTY
You open it.

TANYA
It's your bag.

SHORTY
It's your problem.

A long beat. Somewhere, a pipe drips. Tanya crosses. Crouches. Unzips the duffel.

Stacks of bills, rubber-banded. She picks one up. Fans it. Looks up.

TANYA
It's short.

SHORTY
It's what Dominic gave me.

TANYA
Dominic gave you forty. This is thirty-one.

SHORTY
Then Dominic's counting differently than he used to.

TANYA
Or you are.

SHORTY
Careful.

TANYA
I'm always careful. That's why I'm still alive.

Shorty smiles, without warmth.

SHORTY
You know why they call me Shorty?

TANYA
Because you're not.

SHORTY
Because I was always short on patience.

He stands. The crate scrapes. Tanya doesn't rise. She keeps the money in her hand.

TANYA
Sit down, Shorty.

SHORTY
No.

TANYA
Sit down.

SHORTY
You think I took nine thousand?

TANYA
I think someone did.

SHORTY
Then count it again. Slowly. Like your mother taught you.

TANYA
Leave my mother out of it.

SHORTY
She'd say the same thing. She'd say, Tanya, count again.

Tanya's jaw sets.

TANYA
You knew my mother?

SHORTY
Everybody knew your mother. She bowled a 280 in this very building.

TANYA
She bowled a 279.

SHORTY
She told me 280.

TANYA
She lied to you.

SHORTY
Maybe she did. Maybe that's what she did to everyone.

Tanya rises. The flashlight clicks on. She shines it right into his eyes.

TANYA
Where is the nine, Shorty?

SHORTY
Turn that off.

TANYA
Where is it?

SHORTY
Turn it off!

She doesn't. He swings the duffel. It catches her across the shoulder. She stumbles. The flashlight clatters, spinning light across the machines.

He goes for her. She meets him. They crash into a pinsetter. A hundred pins rattle in their tubes like startled bones.

SHORTY (CONT'D)
(strangled)
You think Dominic's your friend?

TANYA
I think he's my boss!

SHORTY
He's a liar!

TANYA
So are you!

She twists, drives an elbow into his ribs. He folds. She pins him against the machine, forearm across his throat.

Pins spill. They roll across the floor. One strikes the dead flashlight and stops.

TANYA (CONT'D)
Nine thousand. Where.

Shorty's eyes flick to the next machine. To a panel, slightly ajar.

She sees it. Lets him go. He slides down the metal, wheezing.

SHORTY
You were always sharp. Your mother was too.

Tanya opens the panel. Inside: a gym bag. She unzips it. Money. Lots of it.

TANYA
This isn't nine.

SHORTY
No.

TANYA
This is all of it.

SHORTY
All of Dominic's.

She turns slowly, bag in hand.

TANYA
Shorty.

SHORTY
Tanya.

TANYA
What did you do?

Shorty reaches inside his satin jacket. Slowly. Tanya freezes. He pulls out a small black phone, holds it up.

SHORTY
I called him an hour ago.

Far off, a car door. Another. Heavy steps on the lane boards beyond the wall.

Tanya drops the bag.

TANYA
You rat.

SHORTY
(softly)
Short on patience, remember?

The door to the pinsetter room crashes open.
